course: chemistry
lesson: 34
title: Water: the most amazing liquid
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Water looks plain, but it is a very strange liquid. Its tiny molecules pull hard on each other, and at the surface that pull makes a stretchy "skin" strong enough for a pond skater to walk on.
explain: The same pull makes water climb: dip the corner of a paper towel into juice and watch the colour creep upward, against gravity, as water clings to the fibres and drags more water behind it.
explain: Most solids sink in their own liquid, but ice floats in your drink. When water freezes its molecules spread out into an open pattern, so ice is lighter than the same amount of liquid water.
explain: Water dissolves more things than almost any other liquid, so water in nature is never quite pure: the sea is salty, tap water carries minerals, and that is why a kettle grows white limescale.
fact: Tap water is cleaned before it reaches you: it is left to settle, filtered, and treated with a little chlorine to kill germs; many places in the world still lack clean water.
fact: Water molecules pull tightly on each other at the surface, making surface tension; pond skaters walk on it and a paperclip laid gently can float.
fact: Water climbs up a paper towel because it clings to the fibres and pulls more water up behind it; plants use this kind of climbing, with other forces, to lift water.
fact: Ice floats because water spreads out when it freezes, so ice is lighter than the same amount of liquid water; most solids sink in their own liquid.
fact: Rivers carry tiny amounts of salt from rocks into the sea; water evaporates from the sea but the salt stays behind, so the sea is salty.
fact: Drinking seawater makes you more thirsty, because the body needs extra water to get rid of the salt.
fact: In the water cycle water evaporates, rises, cools and condenses into clouds, then falls as rain; the same water goes round and round, so the water you drink may once have been drunk by a dinosaur.
fact: About 60 parts in 100 of an adult's body is water; it carries food and waste around the body, and we lose water by breathing, sweating and weeing.
fact: Boiling water kills most germs, but it does not take out salt or harmful chemicals.
fact: Hard water has minerals dissolved from chalk and limestone rocks; when it is heated the minerals come out as white limescale on kettles and taps.
fact: Water dissolves more substances than almost any other liquid, because its molecules have slightly charged ends that pull particles apart; that is why it is called the universal solvent.
exercise: How is tap water made safe to drink? | It is settled, filtered and given a little chlorine | It is boiled for hours in huge pans at the waterworks | It is frozen and melted again | It is mixed with sea water
exercise: How can a pond skater walk on water? | Surface tension makes a stretchy skin on top | Its feet are full of tiny balloons of air that float | It runs too fast to sink | The water is thicker at the top
exercise: How can a steel paperclip float on water? | Laid gently, it rests on the surface skin | Steel is much lighter than the same amount of water | Paperclips are full of air | Water pushes up only on metal
exercise: Why does water climb up a paper towel? | It clings to the fibres and pulls more water up | The paper sucks it up just like a little pump does | Warm air blows the water upward | Gravity is weaker inside paper
exercise: Why does ice float in a drink? | Water spreads out when it freezes, so ice is lighter | Ice is full of tiny air bubbles that hold it up like floats | The drink pushes cold things upward | Ice is pulled up by the cold air
exercise: Why is the sea salty? | Rivers bring salt from rocks and it stays when water evaporates | Fish make salt as they swim | Salt rains down from the clouds into the sea every single stormy day | The sea floor is made of pure salt
exercise: Why does drinking seawater make you thirstier? | The body needs extra water to get rid of the salt | Seawater is much warmer than the water from the tap | Salt water goes straight to the lungs | Seawater has no water in it
exercise: Where does rain come from? | Water that evaporated and condensed into clouds | Water that falls from space | Water pumped high up into the sky by trees at night | Water squeezed out of the wind
exercise: How much of an adult's body is water? | About sixty parts in a hundred | About five parts in every hundred | Almost none of it | All of it except bones
exercise: Name ways your body loses water. | Breathing, sweating and weeing | Only by crying when you are sad | Only through the hair | Only in the summer
exercise: What does boiling water do? | Kills most germs | Takes out all the salt | Removes all chemicals | Makes it into sea water
exercise: Why does a kettle grow white crust in some towns? | Hard water leaves minerals when heated | The kettle's own metal is slowly turning to salt | Germs in the water grow white | The heat makes white paint flake off
exercise: Where do the minerals in hard water come from? | Chalk and limestone rocks the rain soaks through | Plastic pipes in the house | The air above the river | Salt that people add at home to all their tap water
exercise: Why is water called the universal solvent? | It dissolves more things than almost any liquid | It can put out every single kind of fire there is | It is found on every planet | It freezes faster than other liquids
exercise: Why is water in nature hardly ever pure? | It dissolves things from everything it touches | It is always mixed with a little oil from the ground | Fish make it dirty | Clouds add sugar to it
exercise: Could the water you drink once have been drunk by a dinosaur? | Yes, the same water goes round the water cycle | No, all the water on Earth is brand new every day | No, dinosaurs drank a different liquid | Yes, but only bottled water
test: Josh: "ice floats cos it's got air in it, that's all". What's the real reason? | Water spreads out when it freezes | He's right, only air bubbles do it | Ice is pulled up to the top by the cold | Ice is made of lighter water from clouds
test: If ice sank, what might happen to ponds in a cold winter? | They could freeze from the bottom up | Fish would be much warmer than they are now | Ponds would never freeze at all in winter | Ice would float to the bottom faster
test: Stranded on a raft in a film, the hero drinks seawater "to stay alive". What's true? | Seawater would make him thirstier | Seawater is fine if you're thirsty | Seawater is safe once it is cold enough | Seawater is better than rain water
test: "just boil the sea water then, sorted". Why doesn't that make it drinkable? | Boiling kills germs but leaves the salt | Boiling turns all of the salt into sugar instead | Boiling makes the salt float away | Boiling makes it into tap water
test: Gran says limescale in the kettle means the water is dirty. What's true? | It is minerals from rocks, not dirt | She's right, it is dirt and mud from old pipes | It is germs in the pipes that turned white | It is soap someone left in the water
test: You drop washing-up liquid near a floating paperclip and it sinks. Why? | Soap weakens the surface skin | Soap makes the paperclip heavier | Soap makes the water much colder | Soap pulls the clip down by magnet
test: Pond skater in a cartoon walks on water by magic. What's the real trick? | It stands on the water's surface skin | It flaps its tiny wings very fast to hover | Its feet are full of helium | It walks on hidden floating leaves
test: "why're plants not dry at the top, how does the water get up there?" | Water climbs up thin tubes, clinging and pulling | The plant drinks rain only through leaves | Roots squirt water up like a hose | The Sun pulls water up by gravity
test: Someone says "pure water is everywhere in nature". What's true? | Water dissolves things, so nature's water is rarely pure | Yes, rivers are always pure water | Yes, the sea is pure water with fish | Only rain is impure in nature
test: You run a race in the heat and feel dizzy. Why does drinking water help? | Your body lost water by sweating | Water gives your muscles new sugar | Water cools down the air around you | Water stops you sweating any more
test: If you sealed a jar of water and left it in the sun, why would drops form on the lid? | Water evaporates and condenses on the cooler lid | The lid sweats like a person does | Rain gets in through the glass | The lid melts in the sunshine
exam: Identify the principal reason for which ice floats upon liquid water. | Water expands upon freezing and becomes less dense | Ice contains a large proportion of trapped air | Liquid water exerts a magnetic force on ice | Ice is composed of a lighter variety of water
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of boiling upon drinking water? | Most germs are destroyed, but dissolved salt remains | All dissolved salts are removed entirely | All chemical contaminants are eliminated | The water is converted into a different substance
exam: It is given that hard water is heated in a kettle. Select the observation consistent with the established facts. | A white deposit of limescale accumulates | The water turns a pale blue in colour | The kettle becomes magnetic | The water ceases to boil entirely and remains cold
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which the ocean is saline. | Salts carried from rocks by rivers remain when water evaporates | Marine animals secrete salt continuously | Salt is deposited from the clouds by rain | The ocean floor consists of pure salt
